<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Martin Bucer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1491">1491</a>–<a href="/years/1551">1551</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Martin Bucer was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican friar</a> from Sélestat who became the leading reformer of Strasbourg from 1523. He sought agreement between the Lutheran and Swiss reformers, notably in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> Concord of 1536, and influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a>, who worked with him in Strasbourg. Refusing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> Interim, he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1549 and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/martin-bucer.jpg" alt="Martin Bucer" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacques de Boucq · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Martin_Bucer_drawing.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Martin Bucer was born on 11 November 1491 in Sélestat, a free imperial city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>. His father and grandfather, both called Claus Butzer, were coopers. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> school of Sélestat he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican Order</a> as a novice in 1507, and he later claimed that his grandfather had forced him into it. He was ordained deacon in 1510 and by 1515 was studying theology at the Dominican monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>. In January 1517 he enrolled at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-university">University of Heidelberg</a>, where humanism influenced him. In 1518 he was influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> and had his monastic vows annulled, and he then worked for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> with the support of Franz von Sickingen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His efforts at Wissembourg led to his excommunication, and he arrived in Strasbourg without means in 1523. The council was under the influence of Matthew Zell. On 24 August 1523 the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> appointed Bucer pastor of St Aurelia's Church, and the council accepted his application for citizenship a month later. He joined Zell, Wolfgang Capito and Caspar Hedio. From 1524 he tried to mediate between Luther and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a>. In 1530 he wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetrapolitan-confession">Tetrapolitan Confession</a>, adopted by Strasbourg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstanz">Konstanz</a>, Memmingen and Lindau. Strasbourg suspended the mass on 20 February 1529.</p><p>By 1534 he was a leading figure in the German Reformation and sought agreement between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a>, the south German cities and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, with talks with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Philipp Melanchthon</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> in December 1534. In 1538 Philip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> commissioned him to draw up a policy on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, and Bucer recommended stricter limits than Philip's draft; the ordinance of 1539 was a compromise, and many Jews left Hesse. From 1542 he led church reform at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> for the archbishop-elector Hermann von Wied of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the Schmalkaldic War Strasbourg surrendered to the imperial army on 21 March 1547, and the Protestants retreated within the Empire. Charles V sought the endorsement of a leading reformer for the Augsburg Interim, which imposed Catholic rites throughout the Empire, and chose Bucer. In 1549 he accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-cranmer">Thomas Cranmer</a>'s invitation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, arriving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 25 April, and became Regius Professor of Divinity at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>. He influenced the Edwardine Ordinals and the second revision of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-common-prayer">Book of Common Prayer</a>. He died in Cambridge on 28 February 1551, aged 59. His ministry did not lead to a new denomination, but many Protestant denominations claim him, and he is remembered as an early pioneer of ecumenism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Martin Bucer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Zurich Zwingli’s work was carried on after his death in battle at Kappel in 1531 by Heinrich Bullinger, and in Strasbourg <b>Martin Bucer</b> worked to reconcile the Lutheran and Swiss reformers before ending his life in exile in Cambridge.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · People</a></li><li><blockquote>It adopted the Reformation in the 1520s under <b>Martin Bucer</b>, while Jacob Sturm led its diplomacy and Johannes Sturm founded its Gymnasium.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-strasbourg">Free Imperial City of Strasbourg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sélestat, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.26,7.45&amp;year=1530" aria-label="Sélestat on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4723 476 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Martin Bucer"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5173 746h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5173 746h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.26,7.45&amp;year=1530">What was here in 1530? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
